course: dictionary
lesson: 7020
title: Parody: a funny copy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A parody is a funny copy of something well known, such as a poem, a song or a story. It keeps the shape and rhythm of the original but changes the words, so the copy makes people laugh. «How doth the little crocodile» is a parody of Isaac Watts's poem «How doth the little busy bee».
explain: Look for clues. A parody sounds like a famous piece you already know, but the words are silly or twisted. «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» keeps the tune of «Twinkle, twinkle, little star» but swaps in a bat, and the joke is in the swap.
explain: Parody can also mean a very bad, unfair version of something, as in «a parody of justice». Here the word is not funny at all: it means something that pretends to be fair but is really unfair. A parody account is a joke account that copies a real name, so read carefully before you believe it.
fact: A parody is a funny copy that exaggerates something to make people laugh or to make fun of it.
fact: To parody something means to copy it in a funny, exaggerated way.
fact: «Parody» can also mean a very bad, unfair version of something, as in «a parody of justice».
fact: «You are old, Father William» is a parody of a serious poem by Robert Southey, where an old man gives wise advice; in Carroll's version he stands on his head and turns back-somersaults.
fact: A parody copies the shape and rhythm of a well-known poem or song but changes the words to make it funny.
exercise: What is a parody? | A map of a town | A serious poem that gives wise advice | *A funny copy of a song, story or poem that exaggerates it | A very long and sad story
exercise: «How doth the little crocodile» is a parody of which poem? | Robert Southey's «You are old, Father William» | *Isaac Watts's «How doth the little busy bee» | A poem about crocodiles | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star»
exercise: To parody something means to | Tell the whole truth about it | Read it aloud very slowly | *Copy it in a funny, exaggerated way | Throw it away
test: «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» is a parody of which song? | Baa, baa, black sheep | *Twinkle, twinkle, little star | The Wheels on the Bus | Happy Birthday
test: In «a parody of justice», what does parody mean? | A copy that is exactly the same as the real thing | A story about a judge | A funny song with lots of jokes | *A very bad, unfair version of something
exam: Which best describes a parody account? | A poem written by Robert Southey | *A joke account that copies a real name | A letter sent from a court | A real account run by the famous person themselves
